// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// womb/kernel/sync/semaphore.vuma
// VWK Sync Subsystem — Counting Semaphore (PMT-pure).
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// This file is the K8c layer of the VWK kernel: the counting semaphore.
// K8 (Sync) is building spinlock (K8a, busy-wait + IRQ disable), mutex
// (K8b, sleep-on-contention + wait queue), semaphore (this file, K8c,
// counting), rwlock (K8d, reader/writer), and per-CPU primitives + IPI
// (K8e). K8c is the third sync primitive and the first that allows MORE
// THAN ONE concurrent holder — the spinlock (K8a) and mutex (K8b) are
// both exclusive (one holder at a time), while the semaphore allows up
// to `count` concurrent holders. This makes it the natural primitive
// for resource pools (N identical buffers, M worker slots, etc.) and
// for producer/consumer signaling (sema_up posts, sema_down waits).
//
// ── What K8c provides ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//   sema_init(s, initial)   — initialize the semaphore with `initial`
//                              available units. count = initial,
//                              _pad0 = 0, waitq_count = 0. Called once
//                              per semaphore before any sema_down / sema_up.
//   sema_down(s) -> i32     — acquire one unit. Atomically decrement
//                              count if count > 0; if count == 0, bump
//                              waitq_count and retry atomic_load in a
//                              BOUNDED loop (max 1000 — hosted stand-in
//                              for sleep_on(waitq) + schedule()). On
//                              success returns 0 with count decremented
//                              by exactly 1. On retry-cap exhaustion
//                              returns -EAGAIN (-11 = `0 - 11`) — the
//                              caller can retry, defer, or surface
//                              "Resource temporarily unavailable".
//                              (W42 — same pattern as W16S1's mutex fix.)
//   sema_up(s)              — release one unit. Atomically increment
//                              count via CAS loop, then (W42) if
//                              waitq_count > 0 decrement it — the
//                              wake_one(waitq) simulation. The woken
//                              waiter's sema_down retries its CAS and
//                              succeeds (the count was just incremented).
//
// ── What is a counting semaphore? ──────────────────────────────────────────
// A counting semaphore is the oldest synchronization primitive in
// computing — Dijkstra introduced it in 1965 with the PV (P = "prolaag",
// V = "verhoog") operations. P (sema_down) decrements the count and
// blocks if it would go negative; V (sema_up) increments the count and
// wakes one waiter. The classic use cases are:
//
//   1. Resource pool (count = N): N identical resources (buffers,
//      worker slots, disk blocks, etc.). sema_down acquires one
//      resource; sema_up returns it. If all N are in use, sema_down
//      blocks until one is returned. This is the simplest way to
//      enforce a maximum-concurrency limit.
//
//   2. Producer/consumer signaling (count = 0 initially): the producer
//      calls sema_up to post "an item is ready"; the consumer calls
//      sema_down to wait for an item. The count is the number of
//      uncollected items. count = 0 means "no items ready, consumer
//      blocks". count = N means "N items ready, no consumer blocking".
//
//   3. Event signaling (count = 0, sema_up once per event): a single-
//      shot event flag. sema_down blocks until someone calls sema_up.
//
//   4. Initialization ordering (count = 0): subsystem B blocks on
//      sema_down until subsystem A finishes init and calls sema_up.
//
// When count is constrained to 0 or 1, the semaphore degenerates into
// a "binary semaphore" — functionally equivalent to a mutex. The
// difference is purely semantic: a binary semaphore has no concept of
// "owner" (any task can sema_up), while a mutex has an owner (only the
// owner can release — see K8b's ownership check). Use a mutex when
// the acquire/release pairing is by the same task; use a semaphore
// when the pairing is by DIFFERENT tasks (producer/consumer).
//
// Linux has two flavors of counting semaphore in its kernel:
//   - struct semaphore (include/linux/semaphore.h) — the classic
//     counting semaphore with a wait queue. This is what K8c mirrors.
//   - struct rw_semaphore (include/linux/rwsem.h) — an rwlock-style
//     semaphore that allows multiple readers or one writer. K8d's
//     rwlock will mirror this.
// Linux's struct semaphore uses an atomic_t for `count` and a
// struct list_head for the wait queue; K8c uses an atomic_cas loop
// on `count` (the same pattern as K8a/K8b) and a simplified
// `waitq_count: u32` counter (the same simplification K8b made —
// the full WaitQueue from K7e is not embedded, just the count).
//
// ── Layout: Semaphore ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//   count:        u32  // offset 0  — available count (CAS target word)
//   _pad0:        u32  // offset 4  — padding (see "Why _pad0?" below)
//   waitq_count:  u32  // offset 8  — number of waiters (simplified WaitQueue)
//   Total: 12 bytes.
//
//   The 8-byte atomic_load / atomic_cas window at offset 0 spans
//   bytes [0..7] = (count | (_pad0 << 32)). After init, count =
//   initial and _pad0 = 0, so the 8-byte atomic_load returns
//   exactly `initial`. waitq_count at offset 8 is OUTSIDE the
//   8-byte CAS window — modifications to it never race with the
//   CAS on count. (Same offset discipline as K8b's Mutex, which
//   has the same `count` + `_pad0` + `waitq_count` field order.)
//
// ── Why _pad0? ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// _pad0 is the SAME workaround K8a (spinlock) and K8b (mutex) use.
// AtomicCas is hardcoded to IRType::U64 (8 bytes) in
// /home/z/vuma/src/codegen/src/scg_to_ir.rs:3782-3818 — the IR has a
// `ty` field on AtomicCas that the SCG-to-IR layer simply doesn't
// propagate. This means every atomic_cas / atomic_load / atomic_store
// operates on an 8-byte window at the given address, regardless of the
// actual field width.
//
// For K8c, the consequence: if the layout were `{ count: u32,
// waitq_count: u32 }` (no _pad0), the 8-byte atomic_cas at offset 0
// would read `count | (waitq_count << 32)`. After init with count=2
// and waitq_count=0, that's just 2 — fine. But the moment the slow
// path bumps waitq_count to 1 (count=0), the 8-byte value becomes
// 0x0000000100000000 — non-zero, so any subsequent `cur > 0` check
// in sema_down would be true (cur = 0x0000000100000000 > 0), and the
// CAS(cur, cur-1) would write 0x00000000FFFFFFFF, corrupting BOTH
// count (now 0xFFFFFFFF) AND waitq_count (now 0). The semaphore
// would be permanently broken.
//
// With _pad0 inserted between count and waitq_count, the 8-byte CAS
// window covers count + _pad0 (always 0), so the loaded value is
// exactly `count`. waitq_count lives at offset 8 (outside the CAS
// window) and is modified only by plain field writes (no atomic
// contention with the CAS). Probe-confirmed in K8a
// (/home/z/probe/probe_cas_with_pad.vuma → exit 0) and re-confirmed
// for K8c's 3-field layout in /home/z/probe/probe_sema.vuma → exit 0.
//
// A future compiler improvement could:
//   (a) Make scg_to_ir.rs infer the access width from the State<T>
//       field at the cast address.
//   (b) Add a width-suffixed syntax like `atomic_cas_32(addr, exp,
//       des)` that lowers to `ty: IRType::U32`.
// Either would let K8c+ drop the _pad0 field. Same fix would benefit
// K8a (spinlock), K8b (mutex), and K8d (rwlock). See the "Note for a
// future compiler improvement" section at the bottom of this file.
//
// ── atomic_load / atomic_cas / atomic_store syntax ─────────────────────────
// All three are VUMA IR builtins, lowercase, addr-first:
//   atomic_load(addr)                  → u64  (reads 8 bytes at addr)
//   atomic_cas(addr, expected, desired) → u64  (returns OLD value at
//                                              addr; if old == expected,
//                                              writes `desired`)
//   atomic_store(addr, val)            → void (writes val to 8 bytes
//                                              at addr)
//
// The lowercase forms are what the parser recognizes
// (src/parser/src/lexer.rs:629-631 maps `atomic_load` /
// `atomic_store` / `atomic_cas` lowercase to TokenKind::AtomicLoad /
// AtomicStore / AtomicCas). The capitalized `AtomicLoad` /
// `AtomicStore` / `AtomicCas` are CallNode names synthesized
// internally by the bridge (src/pipeline.rs:8705-8747) — they are
// NOT user-visible syntax.
//
// The parser's argument order is addr-first for all three
// (src/parser/src/parser.rs:3084-3116). The bridge
// (src/pipeline.rs:8721-8732) reorders atomic_store's args internally
// to [value, addr] because the SCG-to-IR lowerer (scg_to_ir.rs:
// 3762-3780) expects value first for AtomicStore specifically — but
// the USER SYNTAX is always addr-first.
//
// `s as Address` is a Cast node that is a no-op in flatten_expr
// (src/pipeline.rs:8811) — it just returns the inner ScgExpr::Var(s),
// which holds the State<Semaphore> buffer pointer. The cast is a
// type-annotation hint that documents the intent (use the State<T>
// buffer pointer as an atomic addr) without changing the codegen.
// Probe-confirmed in K8a (/home/z/probe/probe_atom_cas.vuma →
// exit 0 — `s as Address` works as the atomic builtin's addr arg).
//
// ── Wait queue (simplified) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
// K8c uses a simplified `waitq_count: u32` counter (same as K8b's
// Mutex) instead of the full WaitQueue from K7e (womb/kernel/ipc/
// waitq.vuma, which has head/tail/count). The counter is bumped by
// sema_down on the slow path (before the bounded retry loop — the
// hosted stand-in for sleep_on(waitq)) and decremented by sema_up
// (the wake_one(waitq) simulation — matching the W16S1 mutex pattern
// where the unlocker pops the waiter, not the acquirer). In the hosted
// single-threaded self-test, the slow path is never taken (no
// contention — the first CAS always succeeds because count > 0 after
// init or sema_up), so waitq_count stays at 0 throughout.
//
// A real kernel would:
//   - Embed a full WaitQueue (K7e) instead of just the count.
//   - On the slow path: sleep_on(waitq) — mark the current task
//     BLOCKED, add it to the queue, call schedule() to context-switch.
//   - On sema_up: wake_one(waitq) — wake exactly one waiter (avoiding
//     thundering herd). The woken waiter's sema_down retries its CAS.
//
// The simplified count is enough for K8c's purpose (demonstrate the
// semaphore semantics + leave a diagnostic that a real kernel would
// use). K11 (bare-metal parity) will replace the count with a real
// WaitQueue and wire sleep_on / wake_one to the K9 scheduler.
//
// ── Decimal-constants discipline ───────────────────────────────────────────
// All numeric literals in this file are decimal (no 0x prefixes, no
// binary, no underscores). This matches the K8a/K8b/K7c convention —
// the parser accepts hex/binary literals, but the kernel source
// convention is decimal for clarity (256 = "no entry" sentinel is
// more recognizable than 0x100; 2 = "two units" is more recognizable
// than 0x2). Hex literals appear ONLY in this file's comment blocks
// (e.g., 0x0000000100000000 above, in the _pad0 rationale — but the
// actual code uses decimal throughout).
//
// ── State-typedness note ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
// sema_init, sema_down, sema_up all take `s: State<Semaphore>` as
// their first parameter. State<T> is the VUMA primitive-memory type
// (PMT) — a fat pointer that combines a buffer pointer + a layout
// descriptor + (in the future) an arena bounds descriptor. The
// caller allocates the Semaphore via `state_new(Semaphore)` and
// passes it by reference (init-style API — same as K8a/K8b/K7c).
// State<T> params are 1 per function (matches the K8a/K8b convention
// of 1 State<T> param per sync function — keeps the verifier's
// linearity tracking simple).
//
// ── PMT discipline ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// This file uses ONLY PMT primitives:
//   - state_new(Semaphore)              — allocate the semaphore.
//   - s.field = value                   — direct field write.
//   - s.field                            — direct field read.
//   - atomic_load(s as Address)         — 8-byte atomic read.
//   - atomic_cas(s as Address, e, d)    — 8-byte atomic CAS.
//   - atomic_store(s as Address, v)     — 8-byte atomic store (NOT
//                                         used by K8c — sema_up uses
//                                         atomic_cas to increment,
//                                         avoiding a torn load/store).
//
// NO pointer arithmetic, NO `allocate` / `free` (state_new is the
// only allocator, and the kernel runtime garbage-collects on exit —
// no manual free needed), NO `*ptr` dereference, NO `&identifier`
// address-of. The `as Address` casts are no-op type annotations,
// not pointer conversions. Verified by awk-strip + grep audit at
// the bottom of the worklog entry.
//
// ── VUMA has no import ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// VUMA does not have an `import` statement. Each consumer file re-
// declares any layout it consumes (byte-identically). This file
// declares only `Semaphore` — it does NOT re-declare Spinlock (K8a)
// or Mutex (K8b) because it doesn't use them. (K8d's rwlock will re-
// declare Spinlock byte-identically with _pad0 to guard its internal
// state — see the K8b worklog entry for the divergence note.)
//
// ── Build & verify ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//   . "$HOME/.cargo/env" && CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4 \
//       cargo build --profile release-fast --bin compile_dump
//   ./target/release-fast/compile_dump \
//       womb/kernel/sync/semaphore.vuma /tmp/semaphore.bin x86_64 --verify
//   /tmp/semaphore.bin ; echo "exit=$?"
//   → IVE: Pass passed=1 failed=0 total=1
//   → Wrote NNNN bytes to /tmp/semaphore.bin
//   → exit=0
//
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// Layout.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// Semaphore — counting semaphore. 3 fields, 12 bytes total.
//
// The 8-byte atomic_load / atomic_cas window at offset 0 covers only
// `count` (offset 0..3) and `_pad0` (offset 4..7). After init,
// _pad0 = 0, so atomic_load returns exactly `count`. The CAS window
// never touches `waitq_count` (offset 8..11), which is modified only
// by plain field writes (no atomic contention with the CAS).
//
// Field semantics:
//   count       — the CAS target word AND the available-units count.
//                 0 = no units available (sema_down would block);
//                 N > 0 = N units available. Modified ONLY by
//                 atomic_cas (sema_down's decrement + sema_up's
//                 increment — both via CAS loop, never by plain
//                 field write). The contract's `if atomic_load(s as
//                 Address) != 0` self-test check reads it with
//                 atomic_load (NOT a plain field read) — this is
//                 deliberate, to verify the CAS window's value
//                 (count + _pad0) instead of just `count` alone.
//                 Safe in the single-threaded hosted self-test; in a
//                 real SMP kernel, atomic_load is the correct way to
//                 observe the count from a non-holder CPU.
//   _pad0       — padding to make the 8-byte CAS window exactly cover
//                 `count + _pad0` (both 0 after init). Always 0.
//                 Modified ONLY as a side effect of the 8-byte
//                 atomic_cas (which writes 0 to bytes 4..7 when the
//                 desired value is small — its high 4 bytes are 0).
//                 sema_init explicitly sets _pad0 = 0 to make the
//                 "all zero" state self-evident (rather than relying
//                 on state_new's initial zero-fill).
//   waitq_count — number of waiters (simplified WaitQueue — see
//                 "Wait queue (simplified)" in the file header).
//                 Modified by sema_down (incremented on the slow
//                 path before the bounded retry loop) and by sema_up
//                 (decremented after the CAS-loop increment if > 0 —
//                 the wake_one(waitq) simulation, matching W16S1's
//                 mutex pattern where the unlocker pops the waiter).
//                 sema_down does NOT decrement waitq_count on success
//                 — sema_up's wake_one simulation handles that, so
//                 each sema_up wakes exactly one waiter. sema_down
//                 DOES decrement waitq_count on retry-cap exhaustion
//                 (cleanup — we're bailing, no one will wake_one us).
//                 Read by no one in the hosted single-threaded self-
//                 test (no direct check in main — the field is
//                 touched only on the slow path, which is never
//                 taken). The increment / decrement logic is present
//                 and correct so a real kernel that does encounter
//                 contention will see accurate counts.
layout Semaphore = {
    count:       u32,  // available count (0 = would block); CAS target word
    _pad0:       u32,  // padding so 8-byte CAS only sees count + _pad0
    waitq_count: u32,  // number of waiters (simplified WaitQueue)
}

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// Semaphore operations.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// sema_init: initialize the semaphore with `initial` available units.
// Sets count = initial, _pad0 = 0, waitq_count = 0. Called once per
// semaphore before any sema_down / sema_up. The CALLER allocates the
// Semaphore via `state_new(Semaphore)` and passes it by reference
// (init-style API — same as K8a/K8b/K7c).
//
//   s:       the Semaphore to initialize.
//   initial: the initial count (number of available units). 0 means
//            "no units available — the first sema_down will block"
//            (used for producer/consumer signaling, where the producer
//            hasn't posted anything yet). N > 0 means "N units
//            available" (used for resource pools with N resources).
//
// State<T> params: 1 (s). Pure write — no reads, no helper calls, no
// atomics. The 3 field assignments are direct (no byte-packing needed
// because every field is a primitive u32, not a nested layout).
//
// _pad0 is explicitly set to 0 even though state_new zero-fills —
// this matches the K8a/K8b convention of making the "all zero" state
// self-evident. (If state_new ever stops zero-filling — e.g., a
// future arena-recycling change — sema_init's explicit zero would
// still produce correct behavior.)
fn sema_init(s: State<Semaphore>, initial: u32) {
    s.count = initial;
    s._pad0 = 0;
    s.waitq_count = 0;
}

// sema_down: acquire one unit. Atomically decrement count if count > 0;
// if count == 0, bump waitq_count and retry atomic_load in a BOUNDED
// loop (max 1000 — hosted stand-in for sleep_on(waitq) + schedule()).
// Returns 0 on success (count decremented by exactly 1), or -EAGAIN
// (-11 = `0 - 11`) if the slow path exhausts its 1000-retry cap without
// seeing count > 0. (W42 — same bounded-retry pattern as W16S1's mutex
// fix; before W42 the slow path was an unbounded `while atomic_load
// (s as Address) == 0 { }` busy-wait that would lock up the hosted
// runtime if the slow path were ever taken.)
//
//   s: the Semaphore to acquire from.
//
// State<T> params: 1 (s). Reads s.waitq_count (slow path). Writes
// s.waitq_count. Calls two IR builtins (atomic_load, atomic_cas).
//
// The fast path is the common case (count > 0). In the hosted single-
// threaded self-test, ONLY the fast path is ever taken (count > 0
// after init=2, after the first sema_down leaves count=1, after the
// sema_up leaves count=1). The slow path is exercised only by the
// bookkeeping increment of s.waitq_count — which never executes in
// the self-test because the fast path always succeeds.
//
// The slow path's bounded retry loop is the W42 fix. Before W42 the
// slow path was an unbounded `while atomic_load(s as Address) == 0
// { }` busy-wait — identical in shape to K8a's spinlock busy-wait and
// the pre-W16S1 K8b mutex slow-path busy-wait. K8a's spinlock is
// SUPPOSED to spin (it's a spinlock), but K8b's mutex and K8c's
// semaphore are NOT supposed to spin — in a real kernel, the slow
// path would call into the scheduler (sleep_on(waitq)), which would
// context-switch to another task. The hosted-mode stub just spun
// because there's no scheduler yet (K9 builds the scheduler). W42
// replaces the unbounded spin with a bounded retry: 1000 attempts,
// then -EAGAIN. This bounds the wait — a deadlocked or very slow
// producer can't hang the consumer forever, and the caller can retry,
// defer, or surface "Resource temporarily unavailable" to user-space.
//
// The bounded retry loop terminates because:
//   - In hosted mode, only one task is running (single-threaded self-
//     test), so the slow path is never taken — the fast path's CAS
//     succeeds immediately (count was > 0 after init or after a prior
//     sema_up).
//   - In a real SMP/preemptive kernel, the loop terminates when some
//     other task calls sema_up, which increments count via CAS. At
//     that point our bounded retry's next iteration sees count > 0,
//     exits the inner loop, and falls through to the outer loop's
//     re-iteration, which re-loads cur (now > 0) and tries the
//     decrement CAS.
//   - If the producer is deadlocked or very slow, the loop terminates
//     at the 1000-retry cap and returns -EAGAIN (the hosted-mode
//     safety net — prevents the consumer from hanging the runtime).
//
// Wait-queue bookkeeping (W42, mirrors W16S1's mutex pattern):
//   - Slow-path ENTRY: `s.waitq_count = s.waitq_count + 1` (the
//     waitq_add simulation — we're now a waiter).
//   - Slow-path EXIT on success (ready == 1, count > 0): leave
//     waitq_count bumped — sema_up's wake_one simulation will
//     decrement it. Matches W16S1 mutex's "acquirer leaves count
//     bumped on success, unlocker pops the waiter" pattern.
//   - Slow-path EXIT on retry-cap exhaustion (retries >= 1000):
//     decrement waitq_count (cleanup — we're bailing, no one will
//     wake_one us) before returning -EAGAIN.
//
// Note: sema_down does NOT take a task_idx parameter (unlike K8b's
// mutex_acquire). This is because the semaphore has no concept of
// "owner" — any task can sema_up a unit it didn't sema_down. This is
// the key semantic difference between a semaphore and a mutex: a
// mutex pairs acquire/release by the same task (ownership); a
// semaphore allows acquire by one task and release by another
// (producer/consumer). See "What is a counting semaphore?" in the
// file header.
//
// Note: the CAS in the fast path uses `cur - 1` as the desired value,
// NOT a constant. This is because the CAS window covers 8 bytes
// (count + _pad0), and the desired value must include _pad0's high 4
// bytes (always 0). Using `cur - 1` preserves the high 4 bytes of
// `cur` (which are 0, since _pad0 = 0) while decrementing the low 4
// bytes (count). A constant desired value like `0` would only work
// when count == 1 — for count == 2 it would write 0 instead of 1
// (decrementing by 2 instead of 1, corrupting the count). The
// `cur - 1` form correctly handles any count > 0.
fn sema_down(s: State<Semaphore>) -> i32 {
    // Loop until we successfully decrement count.
    //   `success` is 0 until the CAS(cur → cur-1) succeeds (old == cur).
    let success = 0;
    while success == 0 {
        // atomic_load(addr) returns the 8-byte value at addr.
        //   After init: returns `initial` (count = initial, _pad0 = 0).
        //   After one sema_down: returns `initial - 1`.
        //   After count reaches 0: returns 0 → enter the slow path.
        let cur = atomic_load(s as Address);
        if cur > 0 {
            // Fast path: count > 0, try to decrement via CAS.
            //   atomic_cas(addr, expected, desired) returns the OLD
            //   value at addr. If old == expected (cur), the swap
            //   succeeded (we decremented count). If old != expected
            //   (some other CPU changed count between our load and
            //   our CAS), we retry the outer loop.
            //   On x86_64 this is `LOCK CMPXCHG [R10], RCX` with
            //   RAX=expected=cur, RCX=desired=cur-1; CMPXCHG stores
            //   old into RAX whether the swap succeeded or not, so
            //   dst receives the old value.
            //   The desired value `cur - 1` preserves _pad0's high 4
            //   bytes (which are 0 in `cur`) while decrementing count.
            let old = atomic_cas(s as Address, cur, cur - 1);
            if old == cur {
                success = 1;
            }
            // If old != cur, someone else changed count between our
            // load and our CAS — retry the outer loop (re-load cur).
            // In hosted single-threaded mode, this branch is never
            // taken because no other CPU can change count between
            // our load and our CAS.
        } else {
            // Slow path: count == 0 — would block. Increment wait
            // count (stub for sleep_on(waitq)).
            //   In a real kernel: this is where we'd call
            //   sleep_on(waitq) — mark the current task BLOCKED, add
            //   it to the Semaphore's wait queue, and call schedule()
            //   to context-switch to another task. The task would be
            //   woken by sema_up's wake_one(waitq) (W42 simulates
            //   wake_one by decrementing waitq_count in sema_up —
            //   K7e's waitq_wake_one exists, but K8c's Semaphore
            //   doesn't embed a full WaitQueue, just the count).
            //   For hosted mode: bump the counter (waitq_add
            //   simulation), then retry atomic_load in a BOUNDED
            //   loop (W42 — hosted stand-in for sleep_on + schedule).
            s.waitq_count = s.waitq_count + 1;
            // Bounded retry loop (W42). `retries` counts atomic_load
            //   attempts that returned 0 since entering the slow
            //   path. `ready` is 0 until an atomic_load returns > 0
            //   (count is now available — exit to retry the outer
            //   CAS loop). The loop exits via either `ready == 1`
            //   (count > 0 — retry outer CAS) or `retries >= 1000`
            //   (gave up — return -EAGAIN). This is the hosted-mode
            //   stand-in for sleep_on(waitq) + schedule(): in a real
            //   kernel we'd sleep and be woken by sema_up's
            //   wake_one; here we just retry atomic_load up to 1000
            //   times. The 1000-retry cap is the safety net —
            //   prevents a deadlocked or very slow producer from
            //   hanging the consumer forever. Before W42 this was
            //   an unbounded `while atomic_load(s as Address) == 0
            //   { }` busy-wait that would lock up the hosted runtime
            //   if the slow path were ever taken.
            let retries = 0;
            let ready = 0;
            while ready == 0 {
                let cur2 = atomic_load(s as Address);
                if cur2 > 0 {
                    // Count is now available — exit the bounded
                    // retry loop and fall through to the outer
                    // loop's re-iteration (which re-loads cur and
                    // tries the CAS).
                    ready = 1;
                } else {
                    // Still 0 — bump the retry counter. If we've
                    //   hit the cap, give up: simulate removing
                    //   ourselves from the wait queue (decrement
                    //   waitq_count — no one will wake_one us since
                    //   we're bailing), then return -EAGAIN
                    //   (-11 = `0 - 11` — see K7e's waitq.vuma
                    //   header for the "negative literal via Sub"
                    //   discipline) so the caller can retry, queue
                    //   work for later, or surface "Resource
                    //   temporarily unavailable" to user-space.
                    retries = retries + 1;
                    if retries >= 1000 {
                        if s.waitq_count > 0 {
                            s.waitq_count = s.waitq_count - 1;
                        }
                        return 0 - 11;  // -EAGAIN
                    }
                }
            }
            // ready == 1 → count > 0 now. Fall through to the outer
            // loop's re-iteration, which re-loads cur (now > 0) and
            // tries the CAS(cur → cur-1). If that CAS succeeds,
            // success = 1 and we exit the outer loop with count
            // decremented.
            //   Note (W42): we do NOT decrement waitq_count here.
            //   sema_up's wake_one simulation handles that —
            //   matching W16S1 mutex's pattern where the unlocker
            //   pops the waiter from the queue, not the acquirer.
            //   Each sema_up decrements waitq_count once (if > 0),
            //   so each release wakes exactly one waiter. In the
            //   hosted single-threaded self-test this branch is
            //   never reached (the slow path itself is never taken).
        }
    }
    return 0;
}

// sema_up: release one unit. Atomically increment count via CAS loop,
// then (W42) if waitq_count > 0 decrement it — the wake_one(waitq)
// simulation. The woken waiter's sema_down retries its CAS and
// succeeds (the count was just incremented).
//
//   s: the Semaphore to release to.
//
// State<T> params: 1 (s). Reads s.waitq_count (wake_one guard). Writes
// s.waitq_count. Calls two IR builtins (atomic_load, atomic_cas).
//
// The CAS loop here is the canonical "atomic increment via CAS" pattern:
//   1. Load the current value (cur).
//   2. Try to CAS(cur → cur + 1).
//   3. If old == cur, the increment succeeded — exit.
//   4. If old != cur, someone else changed count between our load
//      and our CAS — retry (re-load cur).
//
// This is REQUIRED because there's no atomic_add builtin in VUMA IR
// (the only atomic mutation primitives are atomic_cas and atomic_store).
// atomic_store would NOT work here because it's a non-conditional
// store — if two CPUs both load count=5 and both store count=6, one
// increment is lost. The CAS loop avoids this lost-update problem by
// retrying until the CAS succeeds.
//
// The desired value `cur + 1` preserves _pad0's high 4 bytes (which
// are 0 in `cur`) while incrementing count. A constant desired value
// like `cur + 1` is correct for any count — same rationale as
// sema_down's `cur - 1` (see comment in sema_down).
//
// Note: sema_up does NOT take a task_idx parameter — any task can
// sema_up, even one that didn't sema_down. This is the producer/
// consumer semantic (see "What is a counting semaphore?" in the
// file header).
//
// wake_one simulation (W42): after the CAS-loop increment succeeds,
//   `if s.waitq_count > 0 { s.waitq_count = s.waitq_count - 1 }`.
//   This is the hosted-mode stand-in for waitq_wake_one(&s.waitq) —
//   in a real kernel we'd pop exactly one waiter from the queue
//   (avoiding thundering herd) and mark it RUNNABLE. The decrement
//   happens AFTER the CAS-loop increment so a contended consumer's
//   next atomic_load sees count > 0 first. The `if > 0` guard
//   prevents underflow in the uncontended case (no waiters — the
//   fast path was taken, waitq_count is still 0). This matches the
//   W16S1 mutex pattern (mutex_release's wake_one simulation), and
//   also matches Linux's struct semaphore's up() — up() wakes one
//   waiter and pops it from the queue. The woken waiter's sema_down
//   leaves waitq_count bumped on its slow-path ENTRY and lets the
//   unlocker clean it up — so each sema_up wakes exactly one waiter.
//   In the hosted single-threaded self-test, this branch is never
//   taken (waitq_count stays at 0 because the slow path is never
//   entered).
fn sema_up(s: State<Semaphore>) {
    // Loop until we successfully increment count.
    //   `success` is 0 until the CAS(cur → cur+1) succeeds (old == cur).
    let success = 0;
    while success == 0 {
        // atomic_load(addr) returns the 8-byte value at addr.
        //   After init: returns `initial`.
        //   After all sema_downs: returns 0 (or whatever count is).
        let cur = atomic_load(s as Address);
        // Try to increment via CAS.
        //   On x86_64: `LOCK CMPXCHG [R10], RCX` with RAX=cur,
        //   RCX=cur+1. If [R10] == RAX (cur), store RCX (cur+1) and
        //   set ZF=1 (we use `old == cur` to detect success). Else
        //   load [R10] into RAX (old), clear ZF, retry.
        let old = atomic_cas(s as Address, cur, cur + 1);
        if old == cur {
            success = 1;
        }
        // If old != cur, someone else changed count between our
        // load and our CAS — retry the loop (re-load cur). In
        // hosted single-threaded mode, this branch is never taken.
    }
    // wake_one simulation (W42): if there are waiters, decrement
    //   waitq_count to simulate popping one waiter from the queue.
    //   The `if > 0` guard prevents underflow in the uncontended
    //   case (no waiters — the fast path was taken, waitq_count is
    //   still 0). The decrement happens AFTER the CAS-loop increment
    //   so a contended consumer's next atomic_load sees count > 0
    //   first. Matches W16S1 mutex's mutex_release wake_one pattern.
    //   In the hosted single-threaded self-test this branch is never
    //   taken (waitq_count stays at 0 because the slow path is never
    //   entered); a real SMP kernel with concurrent waiters would
    //   decrement once per release, matching wake_one's "pop one"
    //   semantics.
    if s.waitq_count > 0 {
        s.waitq_count = s.waitq_count - 1;  // simulate waitq_wake_one
    }
}

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// Self-test (hosted x86_64).
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// main: exercise the semaphore end-to-end with 3 sequential checks.
//   1. Create + init the semaphore with count=2.
//   2. sema_down twice — count should be 0 after both.
//   3. sema_up once — count should be 1.
//   4. sema_down once — count should be 0.
// Exit 0 on all-pass, exit 1..3 on the corresponding check failure.
//
// sema_down now returns i32 (W42 — 0 on success, -EAGAIN on retry-cap
// exhaustion). The return value is discarded in `main` because the
// self-test never hits the slow path (single-threaded hosted mode →
// the fast-path CAS always succeeds on the first try, so waitq_count
// stays at 0 throughout and the -EAGAIN / wake_one-decrement paths
// are never exercised). A real caller would check `if ret != 0 { ... }`
// — same discard pattern W16S1's mutex self-test uses for
// `mutex_acquire`'s i32 return.
//
// The self-test runs single-threaded in hosted mode, so:
//   - The fast path is always taken on sema_down (count > 0 after
//     init=2, after the first sema_down leaves count=1, after the
//     sema_up leaves count=1).
//   - The slow path is NEVER taken (count is never 0 when sema_down
//     is called), so waitq_count stays at 0 throughout.
//   - The CAS retry loops (old != cur) are NEVER taken (no other CPU
//     can change count between our load and our CAS in single-
//     threaded mode).
//
// The test exercises:
//   - The sema_down fast path (count > 0, CAS(cur → cur-1) succeeds,
//     returns 0 — discarded).
//   - The sema_up CAS loop (CAS(cur → cur+1) succeeds on first try,
//     wake_one guard evaluates false — no waiters).
//   - The `atomic_load(s as Address)` self-test check (verifies the
//     8-byte CAS window's value = count, since _pad0 is always 0).
// It does NOT exercise:
//   - The sema_down slow path / bounded retry loop (would require
//     count == 0 at sema_down entry — K8e covers this with the SMP
//     contention test infrastructure).
//   - The -EAGAIN return on retry-cap exhaustion (would require a
//     deadlocked producer — could add as a 4th check, but the
//     contract specifies 3).
//   - The waitq_count increment / decrement (would require the slow
//     path to be taken — same reason).
//   - The CAS retry loop (would require SMP contention — K8e covers).
//   - Actual sleep_on / wake_one (hosted has no scheduler — K9 covers
//     this with the bare-metal scheduler integration tests).
fn main() -> i32 {
    let s = state_new(Semaphore);
    // Init with count=2 — two units available.
    sema_init(s, 2);
    // Down twice — should succeed immediately (fast path: count > 0).
    //   After first sema_down: count = 2 → 1 (CAS(2 → 1) succeeds).
    //   After second sema_down: count = 1 → 0 (CAS(1 → 0) succeeds).
    //   Return value (0 on success) discarded — self-test never hits
    //   the slow path, so -EAGAIN is impossible here.
    sema_down(s);
    sema_down(s);
    // Count should be 0 now. atomic_load returns the 8-byte value at
    // s = count | (_pad0 << 32) = 0 | 0 = 0.
    if atomic_load(s as Address) != 0 { return 1; }
    // Up once — should succeed immediately (CAS(0 → 1) succeeds).
    //   wake_one guard evaluates false (waitq_count still 0 — no
    //   waiters ever queued).
    sema_up(s);
    // Count should be 1 now.
    if atomic_load(s as Address) != 1 { return 2; }
    // Down once — should succeed immediately (fast path: count = 1 > 0,
    // CAS(1 → 0) succeeds). Return value (0) discarded.
    sema_down(s);
    // Count should be 0 now.
    if atomic_load(s as Address) != 0 { return 3; }
    return 0;
}
